reducing the time required \nto implement standard features.\n\n• Developed an internal service for centralized email and document template generation, now used \nacross multiple projects. This boosted the quality and efficiency of outbound communications while \ncutting down the time needed to create new templates.\n\n• Created a documentation portal for business clients based on OpenAPI, enhancing integration clarity \nand reducing the support team’s workload.\n\n• Initiated and led internal knowledge-sharing meetups, contributing to the team’s technical growth \nand adoption of best practices.\n\n• Conducted technical interviews and mentored 2 new developers during their onboarding \nperiod—both successfully integrated into the team.\n\nTech stack: TypeScript, Angular, Capacitor, SSR, RxJS, SCSS, Nx, NestJS, Express, Jest, Docker, Git, \nBitbucket\n\nFeb 2022 — Feb 2023 Frontend Developer, Section228\n\nWorked on an outsourced development team delivering projects for clients in e-commerce, enterprise, and \nmedia sectors. Focused on building user interfaces for both customer-facing and internal systems, as well \nas developing analytics tools and web services.\n\n• Built several Angular applications from scratch across different domains, handling the full \ndevelopment lifecycle—from initial setup to production release.\n\n• Upgraded a complex analytics system from Angular 8 to 15, reducing technical debt, speeding up \nimplementation of new business requirements, and improving maintainability.\n\n• Introduced unit and integration test coverage for business-critical logic, helping prevent regressions \nin production and improving release reliability.\n\n• Implemented offline support in an Angular app using PWA technology, ensuring smooth user \nexperience even without internet connectivity.\n\n• Optimized an e-commerce application for SEO using server-side rendering (SSR), resulting in better \nsearch engine rankings and increased organic traffic.\n\n• Integrated Sentry into the project infrastructure, enabling real-time error monitoring, faster incident \nresponse, and improved application stability.\n\nTech stack: TypeScript, Angular, SSR, RxJS, SCSS, Nx, Jest, Cypress, Docker, Git, Bitbucket\n\nmailto:vlad.zavadsky@icloud.com\nhttps://t.me/v_zavadsky\nmailto:vlad.zavadsky@icloud.com\n\nMay 2021 — Feb 2022 Frontend Developer, Mera\n\nWorked on a PBX platform in the telecom sector—a solution for configuring call flows, auto-attendants, \nand managing virtual phone systems.\n\n• Built a visual call flow editor that allowed users to configure voice routing logic without technical \nassistance, dramatically improving usability and reducing reliance on support teams.\n\n• Implemented real-time server sync for call flow drafts, eliminating data loss in unstable network \nconditions and significantly improving the editor’s reliability.\n\n• Developed a reporting interface for call analytics, giving users access to key metrics and simplifying \nmonitoring of call handling performance.\n\n• Introduced versioning for call flow configurations, including rollback and diff functionality, which \nenhanced reliability and reduced the volume of support requests.\n\n• Created a management dashboard for the virtual PBX, enabling intuitive control over numbers, \nqueues, and auto-attendants.\n\nTech stack: TypeScript, Angular, RxJS, SCSS, Git, GitLab\n\nSKILLS Angular\n\nTypeScript\n\nJavaScript\n\nHTML & CSS\n\nGit\n\nDocker\n\nLANGUAGES English B2 Russian Native speaker", 'fields': {'name': None, 'birthdate': None, 'phones': None, 'email': 'vlad.zavadsky@icloud.com', 'salary': None, 'position': None, 'skype': None, 'telegram': '@v_zavadsky', 'experience': None}, 'parsing_meta': {'last_names_ignored': False, 'emails_ignored': False, 'phones_ignored': False}}, 'attached_file_name': 'Vladislav Zavadsky - Frontend Developer.pdf', 'attached_file_type': 'PDF'}
